    "chunk_size_bytes": 1792,
    "chunk_text": "But\u00a0 British author Roger Boniface who\u00a0 interviewed NVPA aces Pham Ngoc Lan and\u00a0 Nguyn Nht Chi\u00eau (who scored victories in both MiG 17s and 21s) was told by Pham Ngoc Lan\u00a0 that \u201cThe MiG-21 was much faster, and it had two ATOLL missiles which were very accurate and reliable when fired between 1,000 and 1,200 yards.\u201d\n\nOnce its Sidewinder missiles were fired, the Phantom was defenseless against the MiG-21\u2019s 30mm canon. The Americans quickly equipped the Phantom with a fast-firing 20mm canon, and established the Top Gun fighter training school so that American pilots could match the dog-fighting skills of opponents flying the highly maneouverable MiG-21s and MiG-17s.\n\nAlthough the MiG-21 lacked the long-range radar and heavy bomb loads of the Phantom,\u00a0 with its RP-21 Sapfir radar it proved formidable in the hands of experienced pilots, especially when used in high-speed hit-and-run attacks under GCI control.\n\nThe North Vietnamese flew their interceptors with guidance from ground controllers, who positioned the MiGs in ambush battle stations to make their \u201cone pass, then haul ass\u201d attacks.\n\nDuring the 1965 Indo-Pakistan War, the MiG-21 did not play a major role as the IAF had only 12-16 of these at the time, and they were not fully operational. But it was the\u00a0 was the IAF\u2019s mainstay during the 1971 war with Pakistan. The IAF seems to have fallen in love with the aircraft, putting it into uses it wasn\u2019t originally designed for. It\u00a0 showed its abilities as a fighter bomber in the 1999 Kargil conflict. By then, several women pilots too, were flying the MiG-21, and this versatile interceptor made its final combat appearance in the subcontinent when\u00a0 a MiG-21 engaged a Pakistani F-16 on February 27, 2019\u00a0 over Jammu and Kashmir,\u00a0 and was shot down."
