    "md5": "bd0d92d9e30a805f5986af15d3459b34",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1940,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. moves to break Sri Lanka\u2019s exam-centred education culture\n\nColombo, Aug. 13 (Daily Mirror) - Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya has stressed the need to modernise Sri Lanka\u2019s education and assessment systems by moving beyond an examination-centred model and placing greater emphasis on skills, creativity, experiential learning and holistic development.\n\nSpeaking at the public lecture \u201c250 Years of Educational Excellence: Lessons from a Sri Lankan American\u201d in Colombo on Wednesday (12), the Prime Minister said reforming the assessment system was a key component of the Government\u2019s ongoing education reforms.\n\nThe lecture was delivered by Fulbright Specialist Dr. Priyanvada Abeywickrama and organised by the United States-Sri Lanka Fulbright Commission.\n\nDr. Amarasuriya said the reforms were intended to reduce excessive reliance on examinations while encouraging activity-based and experiential learning among students.\n\nHowever, she stressed that the Government did not intend to discard the strengths of Sri Lanka\u2019s existing education system, which has produced generations of academically successful citizens.\n\nInstead, the reforms would seek to modernise the system to better respond to the demands of the present and future, she said.\n\nThe Prime Minister also highlighted the importance of strengthening digital literacy among teachers, principals and parents, while ensuring that academic and vocational education pathways are given equal recognition and validity.\n\nShe expressed appreciation to Dr. Abeywickrama for her month-long contribution to the education reform process, noting that international expertise and collaboration would be vital to its successful implementation.\n\nThe Prime Minister also thanked the Fulbright Commission, the Postgraduate Institute of English of the Open University of Sri Lanka, the Department of Examinations and other officials and partners involved in the initiative."
