    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-0806b761-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "daf07408c1184594627f9d7d93a2b7fd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1238,
    "chunk_text": "Re-evaluating Abortion Laws in Sri Lanka:  A Call for Change Towards Women\u2019s Health and Rights\n\nDespite punitive sanctions, daily abortion rates are reported to be as high as 658 abortions a day\n\nApart from objections based on religious grounds, patriarchal attitudes and conventional social and cultural norms harmful to women dominated the debate within parliament and drowned out the opposition outside parliament.\n\nTaking the legal position first, Sri Lanka maintains one of the most restrictive abortion laws in South Asia. Abortion is permissible only in the gravest of circumstances to save the life of the mother. It is punishable with imprisonment ranging from three to twenty years with or without a fine. Legal restrictions are further reinforced by a medical protocol that requires the concurrence of three doctors before the pregnancy can be medically terminated.\n\nThis has become a thorny problem. Restrictive criminal laws on abortion have driven women underground and they\u00a0 resort to dangerous means to terminate an unwanted pregnancy. A vast and open network of clandestine back-alley abortion clinics thrives unregulated, posing a risk to women who are unable to afford private medical facilities locally or overseas."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-0806b761",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-0806b761",
