    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-0806b761-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "547728453926ddf4e21767aa52ce1493",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1653,
    "chunk_text": "This has become a thorny problem. Restrictive criminal laws on abortion have driven women underground and they\u00a0 resort to dangerous means to terminate an unwanted pregnancy. A vast and open network of clandestine back-alley abortion clinics thrives unregulated, posing a risk to women who are unable to afford private medical facilities locally or overseas.\n\nEfforts to reform the abortion law have been undertaken since the 1970s, yet all have met the same futile end.\u00a0 Following a resurgence in women\u2019s rights with the ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (CEDAW) in 1981 and the Women\u2019s Charter in 1993, several amendments to the criminal law along these liberal lines were introduced. Therefore, in 1995, a draft amendment to the penal code to decriminalise abortion in specific circumstances such as rape, incest, and congenital abnormalities incompatible with life, along with additional conditions for the authorisation of the medical procedure, was presented to parliament only to be inauspiciously withdrawn due to heated opposition, particularly from religious minority groups.\n\nApart from objections based on religious grounds, patriarchal attitudes and conventional social and cultural norms harmful to women dominated the debate within parliament and drowned out the opposition outside parliament. The fear that decriminalisation would open the \u2018floodgates\u2019, encourage promiscuity and indecency, especially among the young, and lead to the breakdown of traditional power structures within the family and generally impose severe consequences on \u2018our culture\u2019 prevailed."
  },
