    "description": "Re-evaluating Abortion Laws in Sri Lanka:  A Call for Change Towards Women\u2019s Health and Rights",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Re-evaluating-Abortion-Laws-in-Sri-Lanka-A-Call-for-Change-Towards-Womens-Health-and-Rights/172-348074",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786641436.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-0806b761-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "59d9adfae9ff922629809a59cc2f8c25",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1448,
    "chunk_text": "Therefore, since its inception, the abortion debate has been centred on two grounds: decriminalisation and legalisation of abortion, recognising women\u2019s right to bodily autonomy and improving public and maternal health by managing maternal deaths arising out of abortions or expanding family planning and reproductive health programmes to prevent unwanted pregnancies. The outcome is a piecemeal approach to legal and health reforms that undermines both objectives.\n\nOn one hand, sidelining abortion as a human rights issue and, on the other, viewing it through a clinical lens, pose challenges to legal reform. The most recent proposal by three medical colleges in 2025 exemplifies this. The proposal represents a setback in the legal reform trajectory of abortion, as it recognises unviable foetal abnormality as the only ground for a medical termination of pregnancy. This discussion thus far has excluded broader consultation with key stakeholders, including civil society organisations and the Ministry of Women and Child Affairs. The lack of consultation and the exclusion of women from the decision-making process, which undermine good governance and accountability, pose another challenge to achieving a breakthrough for legal reform in the country.\n\nWhile successive governments have promoted the concept of reform, there is now a clear opportunity to translate these ideas into actionable initiatives that can lead to meaningful change."
  },
