    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "354741c3f76edfd89d33ea4380f6e40e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "Another issue raised by the engineer is the decision to restrict the tender to local bidders and not permit foreign contractors or local\u2013foreign\n\nHe questioned whether opening the tender to international companies, particularly through partnerships with local contractors, could widen the pool of bidders.\n\nHe added that international participation could also allow local companies that may not independently meet certain experience requirements to participate alongside experienced international construction companies.\n\nThe engineer said the RDA should disclose whether it had assessed the likely number of contractors that would qualify before finalising the\n\n\u201cThe most important question is very simple: when the RDA prepared these criteria, did it know how many companies in Sri Lanka could actually meet them? If the answer is six, then why are we designing a Rs. 235 billion tender with 12 packages around a pool of six contractors?\u201d he questioned.\n\nHe said the RDA should also explain what steps it had taken to broaden participation.\n\nAmong the questions that should be answered, he said, were how many contractors the RDA expects to qualify, why foreign contractors have been excluded, why local\u2013foreign joint ventures have not been permitted and what measures have been taken to increase competition.\n\nWith the sale of bidding documents continuing until August 25 and bids closing on August 28, the engineer said there was still an opportunity for the authorities to review the situation.\n\n\u201cThere is still time. Before the tender closes, the Government can look at whether the criteria are producing the level of competition required for a project of this magnitude. This is public money. There should be no hesitation in asking whether we are getting enough bidders and the best possible price,\u201d he said.\n\nHe stressed that restricting participation could be justified where a project genuinely required highly specialised expertise or capacity."
  },
