    "md5": "52ead142c327b21155348184c98f63fd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1527,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThere is still time. Before the tender closes, the Government can look at whether the criteria are producing the level of competition required for a project of this magnitude. This is public money. There should be no hesitation in asking whether we are getting enough bidders and the best possible price,\u201d he said.\n\nHe stressed that restricting participation could be justified where a project genuinely required highly specialised expertise or capacity.\n\n\u201cThere is nothing wrong with having high standards. The Government must ensure that companies undertaking a project of this scale are capable. But there is a difference between setting high standards for quality and setting criteria that leave only a handful of companies capable of bidding,\u201d\n\nThe engineer said the Government\u2019s stated commitment to prudent public financial management would ultimately be tested by major infrastructure projects such as CEP 4.\n\n\u201cRs. 235.5 billion is not a small amount of money. Every percentage point matters. Every rupee saved through genuine competition is a rupee that can be used elsewhere. The Government therefore owes the public a clear explanation of how these tender conditions will protect the taxpayer,\u201d he said.\n\nAs the CEP 4 tender moves towards its August 28 closing date, the central question remains whether the 12 packages will attract a sufficiently broad field of qualified contractors.\n\nFor a project costing Rs. 235.5 billion, the stakes are simply too high for competition to become an afterthought."
  },
