    "language": "en",
    "md5": "278a4b5cee77b7fc6dd282413869273e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "He said authorities had received complaints about people diverting money intended for essential household expenses into illegal betting. In some cases, families have reportedly been left without sufficient funds for basic needs after household income and savings were spent on gambling.\n\nOperators reportedly attempt to lure participants by claiming that they possess advance information about winning numbers or have access to predetermined combinations for government lotteries such as the Mahajana Sampatha. Some participants are allegedly encouraged to pay Rs. 2,000 or more based on such claims.\n\nAuthorities have also raised concerns about the deceptive nature of these claims, warning that gamblers may be persuaded to place increasingly larger amounts of money in the belief that they have access to guaranteed winning numbers.\n\nPerera said the National Lotteries Board faces limitations in taking direct legal action against the scheme. He explained that the Board's legal powers primarily cover offences involving the manipulation, counterfeiting or misuse of official lottery results, while the wider illegal betting operation falls outside the current scope of the National Lotteries Act.\n\nHe also pointed to the absence of the former Gambling Act as another legal challenge in dealing with the practice.\n\nThe Chairman said the issue had previously been brought to the attention of the Police and State Intelligence Service, but claimed that significant action had not been taken.\n\nGiven the limitations faced by the National Lotteries Board, the matter has been referred to relevant law enforcement and investigative authorities, including the Central Anti-Corruption and Crime Investigation Division. The Board is also working with the Criminal Investigation Department and other agencies to pursue legal action against those involved."
  },
