    "md5": "8e356ac90e03ec3274b2e270a6c6e59f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1848,
    "chunk_text": "President calls for stronger Co-operative network to regulate prices\n\nColombo, August 14 (Daily Mirror) - President Anura Kumara Dissanayake has stressed the need to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s co-operative movement and state-owned retail institutions to play a more active role in the market and help regulate prices.\n\nThe President said that, given Sri Lanka\u2019s relatively small market, prices of goods and services cannot be expected to be regulated solely through market competition.\n\nHe called for a strong price regulation mechanism, with Lanka Sathosa and the co-operative movement playing a key role.\n\nThe President made these remarks today (14) during a meeting at the Presidential Secretariat to review the progress of projects implemented under the 2026 Budget allocations of the Ministry of Trade, Commerce, Food Security and Co-operative Development and discuss the Ministry\u2019s proposals for the 2027 Budget.\n\nThe meeting reviewed programmes carried out by 16 departments, self-financing institutions and institutions funded through the Treasury, with particular attention to food security and consumer protection.\n\nIt was noted that 45 mechanical dryers are being imported under a concessionary programme to support farmers in drying paddy and other grains. The dryers will be provided to 45 institutions selected from among 65 applicants.\n\nProgress was also reviewed on the establishment of nine wholesale distribution centres covering all nine provinces. Construction of centres in Kurunegala and Matara has already been completed.\n\nThe President also reviewed the Rs. 1 billion programme to establish rural-level rice processing centres, as well as the construction of a potato purchasing centre in Nuwara Eliya and a grain-legume processing centre in Monaragala. He called for new programmes to support big onion and maize farmers."
  },
