    "md5": "4f88e4c831718e1c4d03ef831b0e3b49",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1636,
    "chunk_text": "The Government\u2019s rejection of the UN\u2019s external evidence-gathering mechanism remains a serious limitation on its claim to be embracing the fullest possible accountability, meanwhile. And this is where the phrase \u2018reconciliation\u2019 needs rescuing from political upholstery.\n\nOnly then can Sri Lanka begin doing something a bit more profound than forgetting its war and blithely moving on as many are wont to do and want us all to do.\n\nIt can begin beating swords into plough-shares and spears into pruning-hooks \u2013 converting the material inheritance of violence into agriculture, livelihoods, education, mobility and human security.\n\nIn the mean time, Sri Lanka is proving \u2013 much to its detractors\u2019 consternation \u2013 that it is actually rather good at the first half or so of the process of reconciliation.\n\nIt has cleared more than a million mines, and brought hundreds of thousands of acres of compromised land back into productive use. It has trained thousands of people including former combatants in the difficult craft of making yesterday\u2019s killing-fields safe for tomorrow\u2019s civilians.\n\nBut the harder half is psychological and political. A defused mine has no longer a constituency.\n\nBut an alleged war crime does. A decommissioned mine can be destroyed and forgotten. But a mass grave raises tough questions and demands a not so easy answer. That is why Chemmani matters so much to the future of the NPP\u2019s national reconciliation project. It is undeniable that an alleged war crime does have a constituency, as well as its victims, witnesses, alleged perpetrators, political interests and competing narratives."
  },
