    "chunk_text": "But an alleged war crime does. A decommissioned mine can be destroyed and forgotten. But a mass grave raises tough questions and demands a not so easy answer. That is why Chemmani matters so much to the future of the NPP\u2019s national reconciliation project. It is undeniable that an alleged war crime does have a constituency, as well as its victims, witnesses, alleged perpetrators, political interests and competing narratives.\n\nAnd this is perhaps where Rosamund Pike enters the picture again. As a sub-political actor or bit player on the larger canvas of a propaganda war, she cannot excavate Chemmani or identify its dead. An actress who visits us for a week cannot reconcile Sinhalese, Tamil and Muslim citizens with a list of grievances longer than her filmography. She cannot by any means reform Sri Lanka\u2019s justice system, PTAs and pustules and warts and all.\n\nBut she can help draw the world\u2019s eye towards a country that has achieved something remarkable \u2013 and yet, achingly unfinished.\n\nThe one million mines are a success story about removing what the war left forgotten underground. The mass grave of Chemmani is a story about confronting what the war left rotting within the national conscience. Sri Lanka needs both: if it is to look back with responsibility before it can leap forward to future prosperity and peace with justice.\n\nBut better not make the beautiful but asymmetrical mistake of assuming that the two are equal and opposite elements. Sri Lanka is now confronting two very different legacies of the war at the same time, the physical legacy which it has handled remarkably well, and the evidentiary and moral legacy, where its track record remains tarnished still, and image and institution building an unfinished task\u2026"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
